course: biology
lesson: 228
title: Cartoon pets and animal myths put right
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Cartoons are full of animals that talk, cook and win every chase, and that is fine, because they are stories, not nature films.
explain: The trouble starts when we carry cartoon ideas into real life and think a cat should live on lasagne or that a bull hates the colour red.
explain: Think of Tom chasing Jerry: real cats do hunt mice, but a real mouse would rather nibble seeds than a wedge of holey cheese.
explain: Behind almost every cartoon animal there is a real animal that is just as interesting, and a scientist's job is to sort the true bits from the funny bits.
fact: Real cats are carnivores: they need meat, cannot live on pasta like Garfield, and cannot even taste sweet things.
fact: Real dogs cannot talk like Scooby Doo, because their mouths and brains are not built for speech, though Scooby is a real breed, the Great Dane.
fact: Real cats are a big danger to small birds; Tweety always escapes, but real birds often do not.
fact: Lemmings do not jump off cliffs on purpose; a 1958 nature film staged it by pushing them over the edge, and in real life some drown when crowds of them cross rivers.
fact: Mice prefer grains, seeds and sweet foods to cheese, so peanut butter works better in humane traps.
fact: Dogs bury food to save it for later, as wild dogs do, and may forget where they buried it.
fact: Fish cannot talk, but many grunt, click or drum, and a goldfish can learn to recognise the person who feeds it.
fact: Real deer mothers leave their fawns hidden in long grass and come back to feed them, so a fawn alone is usually not lost.
fact: Real rabbits do thump their back feet like Thumper, as a warning to others.
fact: Bulls cannot see red well; a bull charges at the movement of the cape, and red is just the traditional colour.
fact: Owls are expert hunters with superb hearing, but crows and parrots solve much harder puzzles than owls.
fact: Real hedgehogs walk slowly and can trot, but cannot race; Sonic was named for speed, not for biology.
exercise: What do real cats need to eat? | Meat, because they are carnivores | Pasta and lasagne, just like Garfield | Mostly fruit and sweets | Only milk, all their lives long
exercise: Which taste can a cat not taste at all? | Sweet | Salty | Sour | Bitter
exercise: Why can a real dog not talk like Scooby Doo? | Its mouth and brain are not built for speech | It is too shy to talk in front of strangers | It talks only to other dogs | It has never been taught any words
exercise: What kind of real dog is Scooby Doo? | A Great Dane | A Chihuahua | A poodle | A border collie
exercise: What happens to real birds chased by cats, unlike Tweety? | They are often caught, as cats are a big danger to them | They always escape, just like Tweety | Cats never chase birds | Birds chase cats away every time
exercise: How did lemmings come to be shown jumping off a cliff in a 1958 film? | The film makers staged it by pushing them | Lemmings always jump when they are sad | It is how lemmings fly south | The lemmings were trained for months to jump for the film
exercise: What do mice like better than cheese? | Grains, seeds and sweet foods | Raw meat | Hard rocks of salt | Only water and green leaves
exercise: Why does a dog bury a bone? | To save the food for later, as wild dogs do | To grow a new bone tree in the garden | To hide it from fleas | To keep its teeth sharp
exercise: What can a real goldfish do? | Learn to recognise the person who feeds it | Talk in a squeaky voice | Remember nothing at all for more than 3 seconds | Breathe out of water
exercise: Where does a real mother deer leave her fawn? | Hidden in long grass, coming back to feed it | Alone for ever, from the very day it is born | On top of a tree | Inside a rabbit's burrow under the ground
exercise: Why do real rabbits thump their back feet? | To warn others of danger | To dig a hole faster | To call for food | Because they are dancing
exercise: What makes a bull charge at a cape? | The cape's movement | The red colour | The smell of the cloth | The music of the crowd
exercise: Which birds solve harder puzzles than owls? | Crows and parrots | Pigeons and ducks | Ostriches and emus | Chickens and turkeys
exercise: What are owls truly experts at? | Hunting, with superb hearing | Solving the very hardest puzzles | Talking to people | Swimming underwater
exercise: How fast can a real hedgehog move? | It walks slowly and can trot, but cannot race | It runs faster than a car | It rolls downhill at top speed, faster than a fox can run | It cannot move at all
test: «my cat's a proper Garfield, I give him spaghetti every night» What is the problem? | Cats need meat, and pasta is no proper food for them | Nothing, cats love spaghetti most | Cats should eat only cake | Cats should eat only grass and leaves, like rabbits
test: «Mia says lemmings jump off cliffs together, saw it in a film» What is true? | That old film was staged; real lemmings do not jump on purpose | They really do it every spring | They jump off to fly to warm places | Only the baby lemmings jump off, while their parents watch
test: In Tom and Jerry, Jerry steals a big wedge of cheese. If you set a humane trap for a real mouse, which bait would work best? | Peanut butter | A cube of cheese | A slice of lemon | A carrot stick
test: «found a baby deer all alone in the grass, gonna take it home» What should he do? | Leave it; its mother hid it there and will come back | Take it home and feed it milk | Shout until its mother comes | Push it gently over to a busier field where people can see it
test: «why's the bull in the cartoon mad at red, are bulls weird» What is the real answer? | Bulls barely see red; the moving cape makes them charge | Bulls hate the colour red most | Red smells bad to bulls | Bulls are scared of all bright colours, but red frightens them most
test: A cartoon owl wears glasses and is the wisest in the forest. Which is closer to the truth? | Owls are great hunters, but crows and parrots are better puzzle solvers | Owls are the cleverest birds of all | Owls cannot hunt at all | Owls can read
test: Sonic the hedgehog runs faster than sound. What would a real hedgehog do if you put it on a racetrack? | Walk or trot slowly, maybe curl into a ball | Run faster than a horse | Fly over the track | Dig straight down into the tarmac and hide
test: «my dog buried a bone in the garden and now he can't find it lol» Is that normal? | Yes, dogs bury food for later and may forget the place | No, dogs never bury food | No, dogs always remember every bone | Yes, because the bone melted away in the soil
test: Count them: Tom the cat, Jerry the mouse and Tweety the canary are all on the kitchen floor, walking like real animals. How many legs? | 10 legs | 12 legs | 8 legs | 6 legs
test: «stupid cartoon, the fish is talking, fish make no sound at all innit» What is the best answer? | Fish cannot talk, but many grunt, click or drum | Right, fish are always completely silent | Fish can talk, but only at night | Only goldfish make sounds
test: 3 cats and 2 budgies live in one flat. How many legs and how many tails? | 16 legs and 5 tails | 20 legs and 5 tails | 16 legs and 3 tails | 14 legs and 5 tails
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a domestic cat ought not to be maintained upon a diet of pasta. | The cat is a carnivore that requires meat | The cat is incapable of chewing any soft food | Pasta renders the cat incapable of hunting | The cat prefers sweet foods above all others
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the widely circulated depiction of lemmings leaping from cliffs? | It was staged for a film made in 1958 | It records a regular act of deliberate self-destruction | It is observed annually in every lemming population | It is a method by which lemmings migrate southward
exam: Ascertain the stimulus that provokes the bull to charge at the cape of a bullfighter. | The movement of the cape | The red colour of the cape | The scent of the material | The noise of the spectators
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a fawn discovered alone in long grass. | It has in most cases been concealed there by its mother, who returns to feed it | It has invariably been abandoned and must be removed to a place of safety without delay | It is necessarily injured and incapable of walking | It has been concealed there by a rabbit
